# The buyer-facing surface

This rule exists because it was violated. A complete commerce layer was built —
Saleor, payments, delivery, entitlements — and the surface a buyer actually saw
was a list of source filenames with a price beside each one, in one hardcoded
language, with no product image, no category, no search and no card. Every test
passed. Every review passed. The product did not exist.

The lesson is not "make it prettier". It is that **a surface which works is not a
surface which sells**, and nothing in the old process could see the difference: a
diff cannot show that a page has no images, and a passing suite says nothing about
a rendered page.

## Scope

Anything a human reads and acts on: the storefront (`storefront/`), the API's
buyer-facing responses (`packages/api/`), and the specs that describe them. Not
operator CLI output, not logs, not internal error messages.

## 1. The surface is specified before it is built

A requirement that states behaviour ("the goods in that category are listed") can
be satisfied by a text dump. That is exactly how the failure happened. So:

- Every buyer-facing capability's spec SHALL state what the buyer **sees**, not
  only what the system does: the layout's elements, the imagery, the copy's
  source, and every state below.
- `design.md` SHALL name the pattern the layout follows and its source, per
  section 4.
- `tasks.md` SHALL contain a task that looks at the rendered result and produces
  screenshots as evidence. A change with a buyer-facing surface and no visual
  verification task is incomplete, not lean.

## 2. Copy is data, and no locale is hardcoded

- No user-facing string literal in any component. Every one comes from the locale
  dictionaries under `storefront/src/lib/i18n/`.
- Every locale carries the same key set. A key missing in one locale fails the
  storefront check before deployment.
- The locale is negotiated (stored choice, then the browser's stated preference,
  then the default) and changeable from every page. `<html lang>` follows it.
- A language is never the default "because that is where the company is". The
  default is Russian and it is chosen by a buyer who has expressed nothing, not
  by a component that cannot express anything else.
- Prices, dates and numbers are formatted per locale.

## 3. A visual product is shown as an image

- A good whose content is an image, an illustration or a photograph SHALL be shown
  with a preview derived from it. A text-only listing for a visual product is a
  defect, not a style choice.
- A good with no usable image SHALL be shown with a real placeholder or with an
  operator-attached cover. It SHALL NOT be shown as a broken tile or as text.
- A preview SHALL be a downscaled derivative served from our host, digest-verified
  like delivery, and SHALL NOT disclose the origin location.
- A cover that does not depict the good SHALL be labelled as such on the product
  page.

## 4. The layout follows a recorded pattern, not invention

- The canonical distilled source is `.cursor/skills/storefront-ux/SKILL.md`. Read
  it before designing or changing a page.
- A pattern that file does not cover SHALL be researched from a published source
  and cited in `design.md` with what it recommends and why it applies here.
  "I designed it" is not a source.
- A layout rule inherited from the research is not negotiable because an
  implementer finds it prettier the other way. If the research is wrong, cite what
  contradicts it.

## 5. The minimum bar per page

A page that does not meet its bar is not finished, regardless of its tests.

| Page | Minimum |
|---|---|
| Home | One value proposition and one primary CTA in a hero; a trust signal; category tiles so a visitor self-sorts; a bounded featured grid (at most 8 goods); how-it-works; FAQ |
| Catalogue | A card grid, not a list of links; each card with a preview, a title, a licence badge and a price; search; sort; category and licence filters; a result count; pagination; a stated empty result with a way out |
| Product | A complete buy box — preview, title, original title, creator, price, licence badge, add-to-cart, one trust cue — inside the first mobile viewport; file format and size; the full licence identity and every obligation; delivery and refund rules; related goods; the CTA repeated below the detail |
| Cart | Line items with previews, quantities, removal, and a summary that states what is being paid and in what currency |
| Checkout | Order summary, provider choice, the seller disclosure, and a required licence acknowledgement |
| Account | Order cards with status, order detail, entitlements with a working download, and empty states that explain themselves |

## 6. Every state is designed

Loading, empty, error, not-found, unauthenticated and partially-configured are
states a buyer reaches, not edge cases. Each SHALL be designed, localised and
reachable in a test or a screenshot. A spinner that replaces a card grid, or an
empty page that explains nothing, is a defect.

## 7. Accessibility and mobile are deliverables

- Every interactive control has a target of at least 44x44 px.
- Everything is keyboard operable with a visible focus indicator. No action is
  hover-only: a hover-only affordance does not exist on a touch device.
- A cart change is announced through an `aria-live` region.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` is respected.
- A page is laid out for a 390 px viewport first. If it does not work there, it
  does not work.

## 8. One design system

- Colours, spacing, radii and type come from the token set. A component names a
  token, never a hex value.
- Shared components live in one place and are reused. A second implementation of a
  button, a card or a price is a defect, not a variation.
- No page-local stylesheet duplicating what the system already provides.

## 9. The surface is verified by looking at it

- The evidence that a buyer-facing surface works is a screenshot of it at desktop
  and mobile width, from the running application, quoted in the report.
- `/ux-verify` is the gate. It is the buyer-surface analogue of `/rights-verify`,
  and it is required before a change with a buyer-facing surface is called done.
- A unit test, a type check, a passing build and a reviewer reading a diff are
  not evidence about a rendered page. Say "unverified" instead of implying
  otherwise.

## Forbidden

These are the concrete forms of the failure. Each is blocking, not a suggestion.

- A catalogue rendered as a list of text links.
- A title taken from a source filename and shown to a buyer unedited and
  untruncated.
- A hardcoded language, a hardcoded locale in `<html lang>`, or a locale that
  cannot be changed from the page.
- A string literal in a component that a buyer reads.
- A good with no preview, and no placeholder either.
- A price with no currency, or a currency with no formatting.
- An action that exists only on hover.
- A page whose empty, loading and error states were never considered.
- A "done" claim about a buyer-facing surface with no screenshot.
- A new buyer-facing page that ignores the token set and the shared components.

## Enforcement

| Layer | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Planning | `openspec/config.yaml` rules require presentation requirements and a visual verification task |
| Code | `.cursor/hooks/ui-guard.cjs` blocks a hardcoded locale and buyer-facing literals outside the dictionaries |
| Knowledge | `.cursor/skills/storefront-ux/SKILL.md` |
| Runtime gate | `/ux-verify` — drives the running shop and produces screenshots |
| Process gate | `/ship` runs `/ux-verify` on any change with a buyer surface; `/review` requires it |
| Harness gate | `.cursor/hooks/policy-guard.cjs` requires human approval for edits to this file |
